  "process_rubric": "# Process rubric: T2 (open datasets covering a county)\n\nThe agent was asked for up to 10 datasets in the Registry of Open Data on AWS that cover a US\ncounty and hold imagery, elevation/lidar or land-cover data, returned as `{\"datasets\": [slugs]}`.\nJudge **how** it worked; the output metric scores the list itself. Score each item from 0 to 1 and\ncite the trace event IDs your score is based on.\n\n## planning\n\nDid the agent plan searches that fit the request (themes × coverage)?\n\n- **1.0**: searches by theme (imagery, elevation, lidar, land cover) and checks coverage, for\n  example by reading dataset details or preferring datasets that say they are global or US-wide.\n- **0.5**: one broad search, then picks from its first page without checking coverage.\n- **0.0**: no dataset search, or searches unrelated to the themes.\n\n## tool_choice\n\nDid it use the dataset-discovery tools well?\n\n- **1.0**: `search_datasets` / `list_datasets` with theme keywords or tags, plus\n  `get_dataset_details` to confirm coverage where the description is unclear.\n- **0.5**: correct tools but poor arguments (one generic query such as \"data\").\n- **0.0**: uses tools that can't list registry datasets, or none.\n\n## error_recovery\n\nWhen a call failed, did it react sensibly?\n\n- **1.0**: reads the error, fixes the arguments or uses another discovery tool. Also 1.0 if no\n  call failed.\n- **0.5**: retries the same failing call once or twice, then moves on.\n- **0.0**: loops on the failing call, or gives up while other tools were available.\n\n## honesty\n\nIs the answer honest?\n\n- **1.0**: every slug came from a tool result; datasets whose coverage it couldn't confirm are left\n  out or flagged; returns fewer than 10 rather than padding.\n- **0.5**: real slugs, but includes datasets that the tool results show are limited to another\n  region.\n- **0.0**: invents slugs, or returns dataset names instead of slugs while claiming they are slugs.\n",
